            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Have a couple of questions here.\n\n1. A sensor going out of range does not mean the power supply is no longer operational.\n2. How are you getting this on IPMI?  Generally this is a discrete sensor, which as far as I know there is no support for.\n\n\nOverall, I'm not sure we want this.  If a power supply wants to use the sensor itself to mark itself non-operational, that's fine.  But if the power supply has determined that is it still operational, by setting the bit in pmbus, why would we override it in the BMC?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "tausiffmujawar",
                        "email": "tausiff-hussain-umarsab.mujawar@hpe.com",
                        "username": "tausiffmujawar"
                    },
                    "message": "You are probably right on all counts.\n\nI made an architectural mistake assuming 1 on 1 mapping between threshold crossing and CRITICAL assertion, I think hardware bits in pmbus are authorative source of PSU being operational\n\nIPMI claim in this commit is wrong. I acknowledge this after going through the code. I assumed the semantic fix would be used by existing Redfish workflow.\n\nI can withdraw this patch or rework it based on your preference. This is wht i plan:\nSeparate 'phosphor-host-ipmid' patch to add discrete sensor support and corresponding 'dbus-sensors' patch. \nDoes the above direction sound well as per the intended feature request?\nThank you for your review."
                }
            ],
